@startuml 02-value-model
!include _theme.iuml

title CtyValue — one class, three mutually exclusive states, and marks orthogonal to all three

hide empty members
skinparam nodesep 18
skinparam ranksep 30

class "CtyValue[T]" as CtyValue <<core>> {
  **vtype** : CtyType[T]
  **value** : object | None = None
  **is_unknown** : bool = False
  **is_null** : bool = False
  **marks** : frozenset[Any] = frozenset()
  ..derived; excluded from init, eq, hash and repr..
  - _deep_marks : frozenset | None
  - _stripped : Any
  ..constructors..
  {static} CtyValue.unknown(vtype, value=UNREFINED_UNKNOWN)
  {static} CtyValue.null(vtype)
  ..queries..
  equals(other) : CtyValue <i>— three-valued</i>
  is_wholly_known() : bool
  has_mark / mark / with_marks / unmark
}

note right of CtyValue
  **The post-init hook enforces three invariants,**
  and they are why the states below cannot overlap:
  # a dynamic wrapper adopts its inner value's
  unknown-ness and null-ness — a wrapper never
  disagrees with what it wraps
  # unknown **and** null → null is forced off;
  unknown wins
  # null with a payload → the payload is discarded
  ----
  The two memo fields are filled only once a walk
  has **proved the subtree immutable**. A memo taken
  over a dict or a list could later under-report
  marks, and a value that has become sensitive
  answering "not sensitive" is a silent
  declassification.
end note

note left of CtyValue
  **Marks are orthogonal to the states.**
  A value in any of the three may carry any
  set of marks. A frozenset and not a bool,
  because marks compose: a value can be both
  sensitive and ephemeral.
  ----
  unmark() returns the pair (value, marks) so
  nothing is dropped by accident, and marks
  take part in equality and hashing — two
  values that differ only in their marks are
  not the same value.
end note

class "state: known" as SKnown <<primitive>> {
  is_unknown = False
  is_null = False
  --
  **value** holds the payload:
  Decimal, str, bool, a tuple of
  CtyValues, a FrozenDict of them
}

class "state: null" as SNull <<plumbing>> {
  is_unknown = False
  is_null = True
  --
  **value** is always None — the
  post-init discards anything else
  --
  there is exactly one null per type
}

class "state: unknown" as SUnknown <<dynamic>> {
  is_unknown = True
  is_null = False
  --
  **value** holds an UnknownValue
  marker, never a payload — the
  marker is where a refinement lives
}

CtyValue ..> SKnown
CtyValue ..> SNull
CtyValue ..> SUnknown
SKnown -[hidden]right- SNull
SNull -[hidden]right- SUnknown

class UnknownValue <<plumbing>> {
  the marker base class; it lives in
  values/markers.py purely to break
  an import cycle
}
class UnrefinedUnknownValue <<plumbing>> {
  no refinement at all
  --
  UNREFINED_UNKNOWN is the
  single shared instance
}
class RefinedUnknownValue <<dynamic>> {
  **is_known_null** : bool | None
  **string_prefix** : str | None
  **number_lower_bound** : (Decimal, bool) | None
  **number_upper_bound** : (Decimal, bool) | None
  **collection_length_lower_bound** : int | None
  **collection_length_upper_bound** : int | None
  ..
  the bool in each number bound says
  whether it is inclusive; all six
  fields narrow independently
}

SUnknown ..> UnknownValue : value holds one of >
UnknownValue <|-- UnrefinedUnknownValue
UnknownValue <|-- RefinedUnknownValue
UnrefinedUnknownValue -[hidden]right- RefinedUnknownValue

class RefinementBuilder <<core>> {
  refine(value) opens one
  ..narrowing only; a widening raises CtyRefinementError..
  not_null() · null()
  number_range_lower_bound(min, inclusive=)
  number_range_upper_bound(max, inclusive=)
  number_range_inclusive(min, max)
  collection_length_lower_bound(n)
  collection_length_upper_bound(n)
  collection_length(n)
  string_prefix(p) <i>— trimmed to a safe prefix</i>
  string_prefix_full(p) <i>— taken literally</i>
  ..
  **new_value()** : CtyValue
}

RefinementBuilder .up.> RefinedUnknownValue : builds >

note bottom of RefinementBuilder
  **A refinement can produce a known value.**
  new_value() collapses when the bounds leave exactly one
  possibility: refined-null becomes the null; a number bounded
  inclusively to [5, 5] becomes 5; a collection refined to
  length 0 becomes the empty collection.
  ----
  Refining a value that is already known is a claim the builder
  **checks**, not information it records. That is what makes an
  inconsistent declaration fail at refine time rather than only
  on the day an argument happens to be unknown.
end note

@enduml
